<article xml:id="week1" label="0001"> <!-- Change label to a random string of numbers for each worksheet. The label shows up in the url of the website and keeps students from searching for other worksheets -->
  <title>MATH 140 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>

<!--START TYPING CONTENT FOR SESSION BELOW HERE-->
  <worksheet>
    <title> Session #1 (Sections 1.1 &amp; 1.2)</title>
    <p>
      Directions: Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts about the questions with some peers around you.
      The Math 140 textbook is free, and can be viewed at <url href="https://odp.library.tamu.edu/math-business-social-sciences/" visual="https://odp.library.tamu.edu/math-business-social-sciences/">Texas A&amp;M Open Digital Publishing</url>.
    </p>
    <paragraphs> <!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Getting Warmed Up and in the Right Mindset</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Use the matrices, given below, to complete the following tasks.
            <md>A=\begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp 7\\ z \amp 4 \amp -3 \amp 10 \end{bmatrix},\ B=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 8 \\ -4 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix},\ C=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \\ t \amp s \end{bmatrix}</md>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <exercise><!-- #1 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Matrix <m>C</m> has dimensions <m>3\times 2</m> because it has ____ rows and ____ columns.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise><!-- #2 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              The dimensions of matrix <m>B</m> are ____ <m>\times</m> ____ because it has ____ rows and ____ columns.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise><!-- #3 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Because the dimensions of matrix <m>A</m> are ____ <m>\times</m> ____, the dimensions of <m>A^T</m> are ____ <m>\times</m> ____.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise><!-- #4 -->
          <introduction>
            <p>
              Circle True or False for each of the following.
            </p>
           </introduction> 
           <task>
            <statement>
              <p>
              <m>A+B</m> is a defined matrix. <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /> True <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp />    False
              </p>
            </statement>
           </task>

            <task>
            <statement>
              <p> 
              The dimensions of <m>BA</m> are <m>2\times 2</m>. <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /> True <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp />    False
              </p>
            </statement>
           </task>

            <task>
            <statement>
              <p> 
              <m>AB=BA</m> <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /> True <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp />    False
              </p>
            </statement>
           </task>

            <task>
            <statement>
              <p> 
              <m>A+B^T=B^T+A</m> <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /> True <nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp /><nbsp />    False
              </p>
            </statement>
           </task>
        </exercise>
        
        <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
          <introduction>
            <p>
              State the value of each matrix entry given below.
            </p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace=".15in">
            <statement>
              <p>
              <m>a_{23}=</m> ____
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace=".15in">
            <statement>
              <p>
                <m>c_{12}=</m> ____
              </p>
            </statement>
          </task>
          </exercise>
          
        
    </paragraphs>
    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 2  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Let Us Get Our Hands On</title>
      <exercise number="1"><!-- #1 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Given the matrices below, determine if the following computations are possible. If the computations are possible, find the resulting matrix by hand (i.e., do the math without a calculator), then check your answer using a calculator. If the computation is not possible, then state why.
            <md>
              A=\begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -1 \amp 4 \\ 0 \amp 3 \amp 5 \end{bmatrix},\ B=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\ 4 \amp -1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix},\ E=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp 0 \amp -4 \\ 7 \amp 18 \amp 12 \end{bmatrix},\ H=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 4 \\ -2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix}
            </md>
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>A+2B</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>3A-H</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>4B-H^T</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
  
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>2BH</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2" workspace="2.25in"><!-- #2 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find a matrix <m>F</m> such that the following matrix equation is true.
              <md>
                \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\ 6 \amp 0 \amp -4 \\ 7 \amp 18 \amp 12 \end{bmatrix} - F = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp -10 \amp 30 \\ 2 \amp 5 \amp -6 \\ 12 \amp -3 \amp 9 \end{bmatrix}
              </md>
            </p>
          </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="3" workspace="2.25in"><!-- #3 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that make the matrix equation true.
              <md>
                \begin{bmatrix} x \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp y \\ 5 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -2 \\ 0 \amp 4 \\ -2 \amp 3 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 10 \\ 3 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}
              </md>              
            </p>
          </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise number="4"><!-- #4 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Given matrices <m>K = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp x \amp 6 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>L= \begin{bmatrix} y \amp 8 \amp 9 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}</m> to answer the following.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>(K+L)^T</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>K^T+L^T</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Determine whether <m>(K+L)^T=K^T+L^T</m>. Justify your answer.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Laura is planning to buy sugar, flour, milk, and eggs for her bakery business. The prices of these items in three neighborhood supermarkets are shown in the table below.
            <table>
                <title>Supermarket Item Prices</title>
                <tabular halign="center">
                    <row bottom="major" header="yes">
                        <cell>Supermarkets</cell>
                        <cell>Sugar (<m>5</m>-lb bag)</cell>
                        <cell>Flour (<m>5</m>-lb bag)</cell>
                        <cell>Milk (<m>1</m>-gal carton)</cell>
                        <cell>Eggs (<m>1</m>-dozen count carton)</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Supermarket 1</cell>
                        <cell><m>\$3.29</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$3.99</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.79</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.99</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Supermarket 2</cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.89</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.99</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.99</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$3.49</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row bottom="major">
                        <cell>Supermarket 3</cell>
                        <cell><m>\$3.49</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.74</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$3.09</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\$2.89</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write a <m>3\times 4</m> matrix <m>A</m> to represent the prices of the four items in the three supermarkets.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              For the first week, Laura plans to buy three <m>5</m>-lb bags of sugar, six <m>5</m>-lb bags of flour, four <m>1</m>-gal cartons of milk, and four <m>1</m>-dozen count cartons of eggs. Write a <m>4\times 1</m> matrix <m>B</m> to represent the quantities of each item that Laura plans to purchase for the first week.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              For the second week, Laura plans to buy two <m>5</m>-lb bags of sugar, three <m>5</m>-lb bags of flour, five <m>1</m>-gal cartons of milk, and two <m>1</m>-dozen count cartons of eggs. Write a <m>4\times 1</m> matrix <m>C</m> to represent the quantities of each item that Laura plans to purchase for the second week.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>B+C</m> and interpret the meaning of the matrix.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If <m>T=A(B+C)</m>, what does matrix <m>T</m> mean?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use your calculator to find matrix <m>T</m> and then determine which supermarket Laura should buy the items from to minimize the cost. (Assuming she only shops at one supermarket)
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        </exercise>
      <exercise number="6" workspace="1.25in"><!-- #6 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Consider the matrices <m>G=\begin{bmatrix} x \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp y \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>H=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ -1 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
              Find the values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> so that <m>GH=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 8 \\ 0 \amp 12 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="7"><!-- #7 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            A company tracks sales of three products (<m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>D</m>) across four regions (<m>R1</m>, <m>R2</m>, <m>R3</m>, and <m>R4</m>). Matrix <m>S</m> below represents the amount of each product sold in each region.
            <md>
              \begin{array}{r c}
              \amp \begin{matrix} R1 \amp R2 \amp R3 \amp R4 \end{matrix} \\
              \begin{matrix} \text{Product}\ A \\ \text{Product}\ B \\ \text{Product}\ D \end{matrix} \amp
              \begin{bmatrix}
                100 \amp 200 \amp 150 \amp 175 \\
                80 \amp 120 \amp 90 \amp 110 \\
                60 \amp 70 \amp 65 \amp 85
              \end{bmatrix}
              \end{array}
            </md>
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace=".5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If the prices of Products <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>D</m> are <m>\$5</m>, <m>\$10</m>, and <m>\$8</m> respectively, create a <m>1\times 3</m> matrix <m>C</m> that represents the price of each product.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Write an equation using matrices <m>S</m> and <m>C</m> that represents the revenue matrix <m>R</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the revenue matrix <m>R</m> and interpret the meaning of <m>r_{13}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        </exercise>
      <exercise number="8" workspace="1.5in"><!-- #8 -->
          <statement>
            <p>
              Suppose matrix <m>P</m> represents the prices for one faculty ticket to three different Aggie football games, and matrix <m>F</m> represents the number of faculty members who attended each of the three games. 
              <md> \begin{array}{r c}
                        \amp \begin{matrix} \text{Game } 1 \amp \text{Game } 2 \amp \text{Game } 3 \end{matrix} \\[3pt]
                        P = \text{ Price} \amp
                        \begin{bmatrix}
                        \$20  \ \ \  \amp  \ \ \   \$35  \ \ \  \amp  \ \ \   \$50  \ \ 
                        \end{bmatrix}

                      \end{array}</md>
              
              <md>F = \begin{array}{r c}
                        \amp \text{Attendees} \\[3pt]
                        \begin{matrix}
                          \text{Game } 1 \\
                          \text{Game } 2 \\
                          \text{Game } 3
                        \end{matrix}
                        \amp
                        \begin{bmatrix}
                          256 \\
                          312 \\
                          542
                        \end{bmatrix}
                      \end{array}</md> 
              
              Compute the product <m>PF</m> and interpret the result.
            </p>
          </statement>
      </exercise>
        <exercise number="9"><!-- #9 -->
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> be matrices such that <m>A</m> has dimensions <m>3\times 4</m> and <m>C</m> has dimensions <m>1\times 2</m>.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace=".75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find the dimensions of the matrix <m>B</m> so that the product matrix <m>ABC</m> exists.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace=".75in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              What are the dimensions of the matrix <m>ABC</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs><!--   %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%   SECTION 3  %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%    -->
      <title>Now Grades Go Up</title>
      <exercise number="1" workspace=".35in"><!-- #1 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a <m>2\times 3</m> matrix and <m>B</m> is a <m>3\times 2</m> matrix, what are the dimensions of <m>(AB)^T</m>?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>2 \times 2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>3 \times 3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>3 \times 2</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>2 \times 3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Not defined
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="2" workspace=".35in"><!-- #2 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A=\begin{bmatrix} x \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp y \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>B=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>. If <m>A+B=\begin{bmatrix} 4 \amp 3 \\ 4 \amp 8 \end{bmatrix}</m>, find the value of <m>x+y</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>8</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>9</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>10</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>11</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>12</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="3" workspace=".35in"><!-- #3 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>A</m> is a <m>2\times 3</m> matrix, <m>B</m> is a <m>3\times 4</m> matrix, and <m>C</m> is a <m>2\times 4</m> matrix, which one of the following matrix operations is defined?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A+B</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>AB+C</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>BC+A</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>A^T+C</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>AC</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="4" workspace=".5in"><!-- #4 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            An auto parts store has reduced the price of three items (wiper blades, brake fluid, and floor mats) in their stores by <m>20\%</m>. The original price at Store <m>A</m> for wiper blades was <m>\$15</m>, a can of brake fluid was <m>\$7</m>, and floor mats were <m>\$25</m>. Which answer below is the correct <m>3\times 2</m> matrix that represents this information?
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{array}{r c}
                      \amp \begin{matrix} \text{Original} \amp \text{Discounted} \end{matrix} \\
                      \begin{matrix} \text{Wiper blades} \\ \text{Brake fluid} \\ \text{Floor mats} \end{matrix} \amp
                      \begin{bmatrix}
                        15 \amp 12 \\
                        7 \amp 5.60 \\
                        25 \amp 20
                      \end{bmatrix}
                      \end{array}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{array}{r c}
                      \amp \begin{matrix} \text{Original} \amp \text{Discounted} \end{matrix} \\
                      \begin{matrix} \text{Wiper blades} \\ \text{Brake fluid} \\ \text{Floor mats} \end{matrix} \amp
                      \begin{bmatrix}
                        15 \amp 3 \\
                        7 \amp 1.40 \\
                        25 \amp 5
                      \end{bmatrix}
                      \end{array}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{array}{r c}
                      \amp \begin{matrix} \text{Wiper Blades} \amp \text{Brake fluid} \amp \text{Floor mats} \end{matrix} \\
                      \begin{matrix} \text{Original} \\ \text{Discounted}\end{matrix} \amp
                      \begin{bmatrix}
                        15 \amp 7 \amp 25 \\
                        12 \amp 5.60 \amp 20 
                      \end{bmatrix}
                      \end{array}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\begin{array}{r c}
                      \amp \begin{matrix} \text{Wiper Blades} \amp \text{Brake fluid} \amp \text{Floor mats} \end{matrix} \\
                      \begin{matrix} \text{Original} \\ \text{Discounted}\end{matrix} \amp
                      \begin{bmatrix}
                        15 \amp 7 \amp 25 \\
                        3 \amp 1.40 \amp 5 
                      \end{bmatrix}
                      \end{array}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  None of these
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise number="5"><!-- #5 -->
        <statement>
          <p>
            Given <m>A=\begin{bmatrix} -3 \amp 0 \\ x \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 4 \end{bmatrix}</m>, <m>B=\begin{bmatrix} 5 \amp y \\ -2 \amp 0 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>, and <m>C=AB^T</m>, find the value of <m>c_{23}</m>.
          </p>
            <ol marker="(A)">
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>c_{23}=-4</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>c_{23}=3x+1</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>c_{23}=x+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>c_{23}=5x+y+3</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>C</m> is not a defined matrix
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
  
  </worksheet>

</article>
</pretext>